# test/ring/atomvm-unsupported.list — AtomVM's unsupported constructs (feature 101, T017).
#
# 🔴 THIS IS A LOWER BOUND, NOT AN EXHAUSTIVE SUBSET. Read the provenance before trusting it.
#
# Engineer ruling Q-GLPNETS17-01 (2026-09-04): adopt the measured dossier boundary AND install
# AtomVM to measure exhaustively — BOTH, both critical.
#
# ✅ BOTH HALVES ARE DONE FOR THE FOUNDING OBSERVATION. AtomVM 0.6.6 was installed on THIS host
# on 2026-09-04 (WSL2 Ubuntu 26.04, Gleam 1.17.0, Erlang OTP 27) and the dossier's result was
# INDEPENDENTLY REPRODUCED — see test/ring/atomvm-measurement.txt, produced by
# test/ring/wsl-reproduce-dossier.sh:
#
#   probe A (gleam_otp)  → "Unable to open proc_lib.beam / module proc_lib cannot be resolved",
#                          stacktrace at gleam@otp@actor.start/1.  REPRODUCED.
#   probe B (raw spawn)  → ran: "Return value: nil" + <<"probe_raw_ok">>.  CONTROL HELD.
#
# BOTH directions were required before anything here could be trusted: both failing would have
# meant a broken harness, both passing would have meant the constraint had evaporated. Two
# earlier runs were correctly REFUSED by that rule — first a Gleam version skew, then a
# packaging artifact ("module gleam@otp@actor cannot be resolved") that looked like a subset
# violation but was only a missing dependency beam. This is evidence, not a restatement.
#
# 🔴 STILL A LOWER BOUND. Only the proc_lib family has been observed. AtomVM's subset is
# documented upstream as narrower still (ETS, several stdlib modules, parts of the process API);
# none of that has been exercised here, so none of it is listed. The absence of an entry is
# NOT evidence that a construct is supported.
#
# PROVENANCE — why this is measurement and not a guess (research.md R3 forbade guessing):
#   docs/research/gleam-atomvm/dossier.md §3, §4.3 records a spike that actually RAN
#   AtomVM 0.6.6 (AtomVM-linux-x86_64-static-mbedtls-v0.6.6, host build, no embedded HW):
#     * a gleam_otp actor build CRASHED with "module proc_lib cannot be resolved";
#     * the raw erlang:spawn + gleam_erlang Subjects variant RAN, byte-identical to Erlang.
#   That is an observed failure and an observed success, not documentation.
#
# FORMAT: <construct>\t<reason>
# The construct string is matched against import paths and FFI targets by
# test/ring/check_atomvm_subset.sh, which refuses at BUILD time naming the construct (C3).

proc_lib	AtomVM 0.6.6 does not resolve the proc_lib module; observed crash "module proc_lib cannot be resolved" (dossier §3)
gleam_otp	routes through proc_lib — the actor build is the case that produced the observed crash (dossier §4.3)
gleam/otp	the Gleam import path for gleam_otp; same proc_lib dependency
gleam/erlang/process.spawn	gleam_erlang's own process.spawn routes through proc_lib; use raw erlang:spawn + gleam_erlang Subjects instead (dossier §4.3)
gen_server	an OTP behaviour built on proc_lib
gen_statem	an OTP behaviour built on proc_lib
gen_event	an OTP behaviour built on proc_lib
supervisor	an OTP behaviour built on proc_lib

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT LISTED, AND DELIBERATELY SO — these are the limits of what was measured:
#
#   * Only AtomVM 0.6.6 was exercised. A later or earlier AtomVM may differ in both
#     directions.
#   * Only the constructs the spike's smoke test happened to touch were exercised. AtomVM's
#     BEAM/OTP subset is documented upstream as substantially narrower than full OTP (ETS,
#     several stdlib modules, parts of the process API). NONE of that was measured here, so
#     none of it appears above — listing it would be exactly the guess R3 forbade.
#   * Sequential BEAM code (a WAM-style bytecode interpreter) was assessed as fine on AtomVM;
#     only the SPAWN primitive needed the raw form. That is an assessment in the dossier, and
#     the one part of it that was actually executed is the spawn result recorded above.
#
# Consequence for the gate: a PASS from check_atomvm_subset.sh means "none of the constructs
# we have measured as unsupported are present". It does NOT mean "this will run on AtomVM".
# The report says so, and the aggregate still refuses on the unread ring.
